- How do you unit-test edge cases (0, 64, 127) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying known bit-hacks to multi-lane data without branching, simulating performance-critical kernels.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting across lanes
- 128-bit to four 32-bit lanes mapping
- Branchless arithmetic
- Endianness and lane indexing

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}

uint32_t popcountPerLane128(uint128 x) {
  uint32_t lo0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t lo1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t hi0 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t hi1 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);

  uint32_t p0 = popcount32(lo0);
  uint32_t p1 = popcount32(lo1);
  uint32_t p2 = popcount32(hi0);
  uint32_t p3 = popcount32(hi1);

  return (p3 << 24) | (p2 << 16) | (p1 << 8) | p0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 64-bit lanes?
- What are the performance implications on modern CPUs with SIMD intrinsics?

