- How would you adapt the approach for 128-bit inputs?
- What are the trade-offs vs builtin popcount on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a branchless, efficient 128-bit bit-count using a proven 64-bit SWAR approach on two halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Treat 128-bit as two independent 64-bit parts
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// C-like helper (conceptual)
static inline uint8_t pop64(uint64_t v){
  v -= (v>>1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
uint8_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return pop64(x.hi) + pop64(x.lo);
}
```

