## Why This Is Asked
Reversing 128 bits tests a candidate's ability to decompose 128-bit data into two 64-bit lanes and apply a branch-free bit-reversal at the lower level.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value represented as {hi, lo}
- constant-time 64-bit bit-reversal using masks
- composing to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(v){
  v = ((v >>> 1) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((v & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  v = ((v >>> 2) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((v & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  v = ((v >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((v & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  v = ((v >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  v = ((v >>> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  v = (v >>> 32) | (v << 32);
  return v;
}
function reverseBits128(x){
  // x.hi, x.lo are 64-bit values represented as BigInt
  return { hi: reverse64(x.lo), lo: reverse64(x.hi) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type if provided?
- What are the trade-offs of using 64-bit reversals vs a single 128-bit table lookup on platforms with broad SIMD support?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to decompose a 128-bit value, apply a 64-bit bit-reversal, and reassemble in swapped halves to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branching.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit bit-reversal via mask/shifts
- 128-bit composite type handling
- Endianness intuition for cross-half reversal

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```
